tests after the persistence boundary is in place. For now, the HTTP calls below verify the complete
in-memory slice.

## Verify through HTTP

For this in-memory stage, disable Flyway and datasource auto-configuration when starting the
in-memory slice:

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:bootRun \
  --args='--spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.springframework.boot.jdbc.autoconfigure.DataSourceAutoConfiguration,org.springframework.boot.flyway.autoconfigure.FlywayAutoConfiguration'
```

In another terminal:

```bash
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"title":"Kindred","publicationYear":1979,"isbn":"9780807083697"}'
curl -i http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books/1
curl -i 'http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books?limit=1'
curl -i http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books/999
```

Observe `201`, `200`, a bounded list, and a machine-readable `404` respectively.

## Ruby and Python orientation

This is orientation, not equivalence. The controller/service split resembles keeping Rails or
Django/FastAPI route handlers thin. MapStruct has some of the boundary role of serializers, but
it generates compile-time Java mapping rather than performing dynamic runtime conversion.

## Challenge: update mapping

Add an update method to `BookMapper` that maps a request onto an existing entity. Use
`@MappingTarget`, ensure it cannot replace the server-assigned ID, and write a mapper test proving
all editable fields change while the ID remains stable. This challenge does not add a public PUT
operation yet.


